== Chapter III

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Showing that the best things work for good to the godly.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

1\. #smallcaps[The] #emph[best things] work for good to the godly. There are eight of these.

=== The Attributes of God

1\. God’s attributes work for good, these three in particular.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. God’s #emph[power] works for good. It is a #emph[glorious] power (Col 1:11), and it is engaged for the good of the elect. Out of this #emph[strong] comes #emph[sweetness] #footnote[Judg 14:14.]. The #emph[power] of God works for good in four ways:]]

#[
#set enum(indent: 4.0em)
+ In supporting us in misery.
+ In supplying our needs.
+ In subduing our corruptions.
+ In conquering our enemies.
]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[God’s power works for good:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. In supporting us in misery: “Underneath are the everlasting arms” (Deut 33:27). What upheld #emph[Daniel] in the lions’ den? What upheld #emph[Jonah] in the whale’s belly, or the three young men in the furnace? Only the power of God. Is it not strange to see a bruised reed grow and flourish? How is a weak Christian able not only to endure affliction, but to rejoice in it? He is upheld by the arms of the Almighty: “My power is perfected in weakness” (2 Cor 12:9).]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. In supplying our needs. God creates comforts when means fail. He who brought food to the prophet #emph[Elijah] by the ravens, against their nature, will bring food to his people. God can keep the #emph[oil in the jug] from running dry (1 Kgs 17:14). The Lord made the sun on #emph[Ahaz’s] dial go ten degrees backward. So when our outward comforts are declining, and it is almost sunset, God often brings a revival, and brings the sun many degrees backward.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. In subduing our corruptions: “He will subdue our iniquities” (Mic 7:19). The Hebrew word is יכבוש, #emph[He will put them under the yoke.] Is your sin strong? God is powerful; he will break the head of this #emph[Leviathan.] Is your heart hard? God will dissolve that stone in Christ’s blood: “God has made my heart soft” (Job 23:16). When we say with #emph[Jehoshaphat,] “We are powerless before this vast army,” the Lord goes up with us and helps us fight our battles. He strikes off the heads of those #emph[Goliath] lusts that are too strong for us.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. In conquering our enemies. He stains the pride and breaks the confidence of adversaries#footnote[οὐδὲν ἰσχυρότερον τοῦ τῆς ἄνωθεν συμμαχίας ἀπολαύοντος. \[Nothing is stronger than the one who enjoys the alliance from above.\] #emph[Chrysostom]]: “You will break them with an iron scepter” (Ps 2:9). There is rage in the enemy and malice in the devil, but power in God. How easily he can rout all the forces of the wicked! “It is nothing for You, LORD, to help” (2 Chr 14:11). God’s power is on his church’s side: “Blessed are you, O Israel! … a people saved by the LORD! He is the shield that protects you, the sword in which you boast” (Deut 33:29).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The #emph[wisdom] of God works for good. God’s wisdom is our oracle to instruct us. As he is the #emph[Mighty God,] so he is the #emph[Counselor] (Isa 9:6). We are often in the dark, and in tangled and doubtful matters we do not know which way to take. Here God comes in with light: “I will guide you with My eye” (Ps 32:8). #emph[Eye] (עיני) is put there for God’s wisdom. How is it that the saints can see further than the most sharp-sighted politicians? They #emph[foresee danger] and take cover; they see Satan’s sophistries. This is οὐρανόθεν \[from heaven\]: God’s wisdom is the #emph[pillar of fire] to go before them and guide them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The #emph[goodness] of God works for good, in two ways. 1. God’s goodness is a means to make us good: “God’s goodness leads you to repentance” (Rom 2:4). The goodness of God is a spiritual sunbeam to melt the heart into tears. “Oh,” says the soul, “has God been so good to me? Has he reprieved me from hell so long, and will I grieve his Spirit any more? Will I sin against #emph[goodness?]” 2. The goodness of God works for good as it ushers in all our blessings. The daily favors we receive are the silver streams that flow from the fountain of God’s goodness. This divine attribute of #emph[goodness] brings in two sorts of blessings. 1. #emph[Common blessings.] All share in these, the bad as well as the good. This sweet dew falls on the thistle as well as the rose (Ps 33:5). 2. #emph[Crowning blessings.] Only the godly share in these: “who crowns you with loving devotion” (Ps 103:4). So the blessed attributes of God work for good to the saints.]]

=== The Promises of God

2\. The promises of God work for good. The promises are #emph[Dei Chirographum] (as #emph[Augustine] calls them), a #emph[bond in God’s own hand.] Is it not good to have #emph[security?] The promises are the breasts of the gospel, and is not the breast for the good of the infant? They are called “precious … promises” (2 Pet 1:4). They are like #emph[aqua vitae] \[strong spirits\] to a soul that is ready to faint. #emph[Cardan] says that every precious stone has some virtue hidden in it. The promises are full of virtue, and especially in four cases.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Are we under the #emph[guilt] of sin? There is a promise: “The LORD, the LORD God, is merciful and gracious,” and so on (Exod 34:6). Here God puts on, as it were, his glorious embroidery and holds out the golden scepter, to encourage poor trembling sinners to come to him. #emph[The LORD … merciful.] God is more willing to pardon than to punish. Mercy multiplies more in him than sin does in us. Mercy is his nature. The bee naturally gives honey; it stings only when it is provoked. “But,” says the guilty sinner, “I cannot deserve mercy.” But he is #emph[gracious.] He shows mercy not because we deserve mercy, but because #emph[He delights in mercy.] “But what is that to me? Perhaps my name is not in the pardon.” He #emph[keeps mercy for thousands.] The treasury of mercy is not exhausted. God has treasures laid by, and why may you not come in for a child’s share? This promise is like a #emph[bezoar] stone.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Are we under the defilement of sin? There is a promise working for good: “I will heal their backsliding” (Hos 14:4). God will bestow not only #emph[mercy] but #emph[grace.] And he has made a promise of sending his Spirit (Isa 44:3). For its sanctifying nature, the Spirit is compared in Scripture sometimes to #emph[water,] which cleanses the vessel; sometimes to the #emph[wind,] which is the fan to winnow and purify the air; sometimes to #emph[fire,] which refines metals. So the Spirit of God will cleanse and consecrate the soul, making it a #emph[partaker of the divine nature.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Are we in great dangers? There is a promise that works for our good: “I will be with him in trouble” (Ps 91:15). God does not bring his people into troubles and leave them there; he will stand by them, and hold their head and heart when they are fainting. And there is another promise: “He is their strength in time of trouble” (Ps 37:39). “Oh,” says the soul, “I will faint in the day of trial.” But God will be “the strength of my heart”; he will join his forces with us. Either he will make his hand lighter, or our faith stronger.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Do we fear outward want? There is a promise: “Those who seek the LORD lack no good thing” (Ps 34:10). If it is good for us, we will have it; if it is not good for us, then not having it is good. “He will bless your bread and your water” (Exod 23:25). This blessing falls like the honeydew on the leaf; it sweetens the little we possess. Let me go without the #emph[venison,] so long as I may have the #emph[blessing. Question.] “But I fear I will not get a living?” #emph[Answer.] Read carefully that scripture, Ps 37:25: “I once was young and now am old, yet never have I seen the righteous abandoned or their children begging for bread.” How must we understand this? 1. #emph[David] speaks it as his own observation. He never saw such an eclipse; he never saw a godly man brought so low that he did not have a bite of bread to put in his mouth. 2. #emph[David] never saw the righteous and #emph[their children] in want. Though the Lord might test godly parents for a while by want, yet not #emph[their children] too: the children of the godly will be provided for. 3. #emph[David] never saw the righteous begging bread and #emph[abandoned.] Though he might be brought into great straits, yet he is not abandoned. He is still an heir of heaven, and God loves him. So in all these cases the promises work for good.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[How do the promises work for good?#footnote[#emph[Question. Answer.] 1.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. They are food for faith, and what strengthens faith works for good. The promises are the breast milk of faith; faith sucks nourishment from them, as the child does by drawing on the breast. #emph[Jacob] was “in great fear and distress” (Gen 32:7); his spirits were ready to faint. Now he goes to the promise (#emph[verse] 12): “But You have said, ‘I will surely do you good.’ ” This promise was his food. He got so much strength by sucking on this promise that he was able to wrestle with the Lord in prayer all night, and would not let him go until he had blessed him.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The promises are springs of joy. There is more in the promise to comfort than in the world to perplex. #emph[Ursinus] was comforted by the promise in John 10:29: “No one can snatch them out of My Father’s hand.” The promises are cordials in a fainting fit: “If Your law had not been my delight, then I would have perished in my affliction” (Ps 119:92). The promises are like the cork on the net, to keep the heart from sinking in the deep waters of distress.]]

=== The Mercies of God

3\. The mercies of God work for good to the godly.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Temporal mercies,] such as health and prosperity. Mercy works most kindly on a noble spirit.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The mercies of God humble: “Then King David went in, sat before the LORD, and said, ‘Who am I, O Lord GOD, and what is my house, that You have brought me this far?’ ” (2 Sam 7:18). Lord, where does such honor conferred on me come from, that I should be king? That I who followed the sheep should go in and out before your people? So a gracious heart says, Lord, what am I, that it should be better with me than with others? That I should drink of the fruit of the vine, when others drink not only a cup of #emph[wormwood] but a cup of #emph[blood?] What am I, that I should have those mercies that others lack, who are better than I? Lord, how is it that, in spite of all my unworthiness, a fresh tide of mercy comes in every day? The mercies of God make a sinner proud, but a saint humble.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The mercies of God have a melting influence on the soul; they dissolve it in love to God #footnote[Διὰ τοῦτο πολλὰς ἐις ἡμᾶς ἐυεργεσίας κατατίθεται ὁ θεὸς ἵνα ἡμᾶς ἐφελκύσηται. \[For this reason God lays up many benefits for us, that he may draw us to himself.\] #emph[Chrysost.]]. God’s judgments make us fear him; his mercies make us love him. How #emph[Saul] was worked on by kindness! #emph[David] had him at an advantage, and might have cut off not only the edge of his robe but his head; yet he spared his life. This kindness melted #emph[Saul’s] heart: “Is that your voice, David my son?” Then Saul wept aloud (1 Sam 24:16). Such a melting influence God’s mercy has: it makes the eyes drop tears of love.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The mercies of God make the heart fruitful. When you spend more on a field, it bears a better crop. A gracious soul #emph[honors the LORD with his wealth.] He does not do with his mercies as #emph[Israel] did with their jewels and earrings, #emph[make a golden calf]\; but as #emph[Solomon] did with the money brought into the treasury, he #emph[builds a temple] for the Lord. The golden showers of mercy cause fruitfulness.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. The mercies of God make the heart thankful: #emph[Quid retribuam Domino?] “How can I repay the LORD for all His goodness to me? I will lift the cup of salvation” (Ps 116:12, 13). #emph[David] alludes to the people of #emph[Israel,] who at their peace offerings used to take a cup in their hands and give thanks to God for deliverances. Every mercy is an alms of free grace, and this enlarges the soul in gratitude. A good Christian is not a grave to bury God’s mercies, but a temple to sing his praise. If every bird in its kind (as #emph[Ambrose] says) chirps out thankfulness to its Maker, much more will a noble-hearted Christian, whose life is enriched and perfumed with mercy.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. The mercies of God quicken. As they are lodestones to love, so they are whetstones to obedience: “I will walk before the LORD in the land of the living” (Ps 116:9). He who looks back over his blessings regards himself as a person bound to God. He argues from the sweetness of mercy to the swiftness of duty; #emph[he spends and is spent] for Christ; he dedicates his τὸ ζῆν \[life\] to God. Among the #emph[Romans,] when one man had redeemed another, the redeemed man was to serve him ever afterward. A soul surrounded with mercy is zealously active in God’s service.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. The mercies of God work compassion toward others. A Christian is a #emph[temporal savior.] He feeds the hungry, clothes the naked, and visits the widow and orphan in their distress. The backs and bellies of the poor are the furrows where he sows the golden seeds of his charity: “It is well with the man who is generous and lends freely” (Ps 112:5). Charity drops from him freely, as myrrh from the tree. So to the godly the mercies of God work for good; they are wings to lift them up to heaven.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Spiritual mercies] work for good: the blessed ordinances.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The word preached works for good. It is #emph[a fragrance that brings life]\; it is #emph[verbum cum unctione] \[a word with anointing\]; it is a soul-transforming word. It shapes the heart into Christ’s likeness. It is the breeder of assurance: “Our gospel came to you not only in word, but also in power, in the Holy Spirit, and with great assurance” (1 Thess 1:5). It is #emph[vehiculum salutis,] the chariot of salvation.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Prayer works for good #footnote[παντὸς ἀγαθοῦ κεφάλαιον ἐστιν ἡ προσευχὴ. \[Prayer is the sum of every good thing.\] #emph[Chr. orat. de prec.]]. Prayer is the bellows of the affections; it blows up holy desires and the fervor of the soul. Prayer has power with God: “Command Me” (Isa 45:11). It is a key that unlocks the treasury of God’s tender mercies. Prayer keeps the heart open to God and shut to sin; it cools the feverish heats and swellings of lust. It was #emph[Luther]’s counsel to a friend, when he felt a temptation begin to rise, to turn to prayer. Prayer is #emph[bombarda Christianorum] #footnote[#emph[Luther.]], the Christians’ gun, which they fire against their enemies. Prayer is the #emph[pancreston,] the sovereign medicine of the soul. Prayer sanctifies every mercy (1 Tim 4:5). It drives away sorrow: by venting the grief it eases the heart. When #emph[Hannah] had prayed, she “went on her way … and her face was no longer downcast” (1 Sam 1:18). And if prayer has these rare effects, then it works for good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The Lord’s Supper works for good. It is an emblem of the marriage supper of the Lamb (Rev 19:9), and an #emph[earnest] of the communion we will have with Christ in glory. It is #emph[a feast of choice meat.] It gives us bread from heaven, such as not only #emph[preserves life] but #emph[prevents death.] It has glorious effects in the hearts of the godly: it quickens their affections, strengthens their graces, mortifies their corruptions, revives their hopes, and increases their joy. #emph[Luther] says, “It is as great a work to comfort a downcast soul as to raise the dead to life.” Yet this may be done, and sometimes is done, to the souls of the godly in the blessed Supper. The sacrament has a special excellence above the word preached. In the word there is the breath of God, in the sacrament the blood of God. In the word we hear his voice, in the sacrament we have his kiss. The word comes out of God’s mouth, the sacrament out of his side.]]

=== The Graces of the Spirit

4\. The graces of the Spirit work for good. Grace is to the soul as light to the eye, as health to the body. Grace does for the soul what a virtuous wife does for her husband: “She brings him good … all the days of her life” (Prov 31:12). How incomparably useful the graces are! Faith and fear go hand in hand. Faith keeps the heart cheerful; fear keeps the heart serious. Faith keeps the heart from sinking in despair; fear keeps it from floating in presumption. All the graces show themselves in their beauty: hope is the #emph[helmet] (1 Thess 5:8), meekness the #emph[ornament] (1 Pet 3:4), love the #emph[bond of perfection] (Col 3:14). The saints’ graces are weapons to defend them, wings to lift them, jewels to enrich them, spices to perfume them, stars to adorn them #footnote[ψυχὴ λαμπροτέρα γίνεται τὴν δεξαμένη χάριν. \[The soul that has received grace becomes brighter.\]], and cordials to refresh them. And does not all this work for good? The graces are our evidences for heaven; is it not good to have our evidences ready at the hour of death?

=== The Creatures of God

5\. The creatures of God work for good to the godly. 1. #emph[Inanimate] creatures: “From the heavens the stars fought; from their courses they fought against Sisera” (Judg 5:20). The stars, gathered like the host of God in battle array, raised terrible storms by their influences, and as it were conspired the ruin of #emph[Sisera] and his army. 2. #emph[Animate] creatures. The angels, those noble citizens and princes of heaven, work for the good of the saints #footnote[#emph[Domestici Dei, caeli cives, principes paradisi.] \[Members of God’s household, citizens of heaven, princes of paradise.\] Bernard]. The good angels are ready to do every office of love for the people of God: “Are not all the angels ministering spirits sent to serve those who will inherit salvation?” (Heb 1:14). Some of the Fathers are of the opinion that every believer has his own guardian angel. It needs no hot debate; it may be enough for us to know that the whole hierarchy of angels is employed for the good of the saints. The good angels do service to the saints:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[In life.] The angel comforted the virgin #emph[Mary] (Luke 1:28). The angel shut the mouths of the lions so that they could not hurt #emph[Daniel] (Dan 6:22). A Christian has an invisible guard about him, the keeping and guardianship of angels: “For He will command His angels concerning you to guard you in all your ways” (Ps 91:11). The angels are part of the saints’ bodyguard, yes, the chief of the angels: “Are not all the angels ministering spirits?” The highest angels take care of the lowest saints.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[At death.] The angels are about the saints’ sickbeds to comfort them. As God comforts by his Spirit, so he comforts by his angels. Christ in his agony was strengthened by an angel (Luke 22:43), and so are believers in the agony of death. And when the saints breathe their last, their souls are carried up to heaven by an escort of angels (Luke 16:22).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[At the day of judgment.] 1. The angels will open the saints’ graves, dig away the earth from their bodies, and lead them into the presence of Christ, when they will be made like his glorious body: “And He will send out His angels … and they will gather His elect from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the other” (Matt 24:31). 2. At the day of judgment the angels will rid the godly of all their enemies. Here the saints are plagued with enemies: “Those who repay my good with evil attack me for pursuing the good” (Ps 38:20). Well, the angels will shortly give God’s people their discharge and set them free from all their enemies: “The weeds are the sons of the evil one … The harvest is the end of the age, and the harvesters are angels. As the weeds are collected and burned in the fire, so will it be at the end of the age. The Son of Man will send out His angels, and they will weed out of His kingdom every cause of sin and all who practice lawlessness. And they will throw them into the fiery furnace” (Matt 13:38). At the day of judgment the angels of God will take the wicked, who are the weeds, bundle them up, and throw them into the furnace of hell, and then the godly will not be troubled with enemies any more. So the good angels work for good. See here the honor and dignity of a believer. He has #emph[God’s name] written on him (Rev 3:12), the Holy Spirit dwelling in him (2 Tim 1:14), and a guard of angels attending him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Insuper nostro lateri ministros, caelitus addis.] \[Moreover, from heaven you set ministering spirits at our side.\]]]

=== The Communion of Saints

6\. The communion of saints works for good: συνεργοὶ ἐσμὲν, “we are fellow workers with you for your joy” (2 Cor 1:24). One Christian talking with another is a means to strengthen him, as the stones in an arch help to strengthen one another. One Christian, by sharing his experiences, warms and quickens another: “Let us … spur one another on to love and good deeds” (Heb 10:24). How grace flourishes by holy conversation! A Christian, by good talk, drops such oil on another as makes the lamp of his faith burn brighter.

=== Christ’s Intercession

7\. Christ’s intercession works for good. Christ is in heaven like #emph[Aaron] with his golden plate on his forehead and his precious incense, and he prays for all believers as he did for the apostles: “I am not asking on behalf of them alone, but also on behalf of those who will believe in Me” (John 17:20). When a Christian is weak and can hardly pray for himself, Jesus Christ is praying for him. And he prays for three things. 1. That the saints may be kept from sin (verse 15): “that You keep them from evil.” We live in the world as in a plague house; Christ prays that his saints may not be infected with the contagious evil of the times. 2. For his people’s progress in holiness (#emph[verse] 17): “Sanctify them.” Let them have constant supplies of the Spirit, and be anointed “with fresh oil.” 3. For their glorification (verse 24): “Father, I want those You have given Me to be with Me where I am.” Christ is not content until the saints are in his arms. This prayer that he made on earth is the copy and pattern of his prayer in heaven. What a comfort this is: when Satan is tempting, Christ is praying! This works for good.

Christ’s prayer takes away the sins of our prayers. A child (says St. #emph[Ambrose]) who wants to present his father with a posy goes into the garden and gathers some flowers and some weeds together. But when he comes to his mother, she picks out the weeds and binds up the flowers, and so it is presented to the father. So when we have offered up our prayers, Christ comes and picks away the weeds, #emph[the sin of our prayer,] and presents nothing but flowers to his Father, which are a sweet-smelling fragrance.

=== The Saints’ Prayers

8\. The joint stock of the saints’ prayers works for good to the godly. The saints pray for all the members of the mystical body, and their prayers prevail much #footnote[Αἱ ψυχαὶ διὰ τῶν ἁγίων προσευχῶν ἁρμόζονται καὶ δρόμον τῆς ἐυσεβείας τρέχουσιν ἐυχερῶς. \[Through the prayers of the saints, souls are set in order and run the course of godliness with ease.\]]. 1. They prevail for recovery from sickness: “The prayer offered in faith will restore the one who is sick. The Lord will raise him up” (Jas 5:15). 2. For victory over enemies: “Lift up a prayer for the remnant that still survives” (Isa 37:4); “Then the angel of the LORD went out and struck down 185,000 men in the camp of the Assyrians” (verse 36). 3. For deliverance out of prison: “The church was fervently praying to God for him” (Acts 12:5); “Suddenly an angel of the Lord appeared and a light shone in the cell. He tapped Peter on the side and woke him up … And the chains fell off his wrists” (verse 7). The angel fetched #emph[Peter] out of prison, but it was prayer that fetched the angel. 4. For forgiveness of sin: “My servant Job will pray for you#super[+], for I will accept his prayer” (Job 42:8). So the prayers of the saints work for good to the mystical body. And this is no small privilege for a child of God, that he has a constant trade of prayer carried on for him. When he comes into any town or city, he may say, I have some prayer here; indeed, all the world over I have a stock of prayer going for me. When I am out of sorts and out of tune, others are praying for me who are quick and lively. So the best things work for good to the people of God.
